-- allium: 3
-- waiting.allium

-- Scope: How Vera shows that something is taking time, and how long it has been taking
-- Includes: Naming the wait, counting elapsed time, remembering measured durations,
--   saying how long something usually takes, and ending the indication
-- Excludes:
--   - Whether the work itself is fast (see offline-reconciliation.allium)
--   - The meaning of the work being waited for
--   - Timers, intervals and rendering libraries
--
-- North star: a number that rises cannot lie. If the work hangs, the number keeps
-- rising, and that is exactly the truth — it is still waiting. A spinning wheel says
-- the same thing whether it is alive or dead, which is why Vera does not spin one.

use "./core.allium" as core

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Contracts
------------------------------------------------------------

contract TruthfulWaiting {
    begin: (work: String, key: String?) -> Wait
    end: (wait: Wait) -> WaitMemory?

    @invariant WaitingIsCountedNotAnimated
        -- Elapsed time is presented as a rising measurement of time already spent.
        -- An indeterminate animation is not a substitute: it looks identical while
        -- the work advances, while it is blocked and after it has died, so it cannot
        -- tell a participant which of the three is happening.

    @invariant NothingIsPromisedThatIsNotKnown
        -- Vera never presents a percentage, a proportion completed or a remaining
        -- time for work whose remaining duration it cannot compute. Counting cannot
        -- be wrong, because the time did pass. Predicting can.

    @invariant BriefWorkWaitsInSilence
        -- Work that finishes below the perceptibility threshold shows no indication
        -- at all. An indicator that appears on every instantaneous step turns the
        -- interface into a flicker of numbers and teaches the eye to ignore it.

    @invariant EveryWaitIsNamed
        -- What is being waited for is stated in the participant's terms. A duration
        -- without a subject says that Vera is busy, which the participant already
        -- knew from the fact that nothing happened.

    @invariant ExpectationComesFromMeasurement
        -- A statement about how long something usually takes is derived from
        -- durations this device measured for that same named wait, never from a
        -- constant, an estimate or another device's experience.

    @invariant NoWaitOutlivesItsWork
        -- The indication ends when the work ends, whether it succeeded or failed.
        -- An indicator left behind is the worst available lie, because it reports
        -- that something is still happening when nothing is.

    @invariant PeripheralAwarenessDoesNotReplaceExplanation
        -- A single unobtrusive frame may make concurrent work perceptible across
        -- the whole interface. It never replaces the local account of what is
        -- happening, how long it has taken, what failed or what can be done.

    @invariant SystemNoticesShareOneMovableOverlay
        -- Transient system messages and the account of active work appear in one
        -- consistent overlay whose corner the participant may change. A message
        -- does not change colour or jump above a page, below its properties or
        -- inside the map merely because a different subsystem produced it.

    @invariant BackgroundWorkDoesNotTakeTheHand
        -- Refreshing the account of work never rebuilds the page being read or
        -- edited, steals keyboard focus, changes the selection or returns the
        -- reading viewport to the beginning. Canonical content is recomposed only
        -- when it actually changed, and waits until an active edit can survive it.

    @invariant AWorkingObjectBreathesAroundItsWholePerimeter
        -- When an individual object such as a Mermaid diagram is still rendering,
        -- its local work signal follows all four edges and their rounded corners.
        -- A detached left stroke is not presented as the frame of a rounded box.

    @invariant MotionIsNeverRequiredToUnderstandWork
        -- The global work signal remains legible without animation. A participant
        -- who requests reduced motion receives a stable indication with the same
        -- meaning as the breathing frame.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Enumerations
------------------------------------------------------------

enum WaitOutcome { succeeded | failed | abandoned }

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Entities
------------------------------------------------------------

entity Wait {
    -- Who is waiting. A duration measured on a phone and one measured on a
    -- workstation are not the same measurement, and neither are two people's.
    participant: core/Participant
    -- What is being waited for, said as a person would say it.
    work: String
    -- How this wait is recognised across occurrences, or null when it cannot be
    -- named reliably. Two calls to the same local model are the same wait even
    -- when their text differs.
    key: String?
    started_at: Timestamp
    -- Below this, nothing is shown.
    threshold: Duration
    ended: Boolean
    outcome: WaitOutcome?
}

entity WaitMemory {
    key: String
    -- The most recent measured durations for this key, on this device.
    durations: Duration
    measured_on: core/Participant
}

entity ElapsedIndication {
    wait: Wait
    elapsed: Duration
    -- Present only when this device has measured this key before.
    usually_takes: Duration?
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules
------------------------------------------------------------

rule BeginWaitingForNamedWork {
    when: WorkBegan(participant, work, key?)

    ensures:
        Wait.created(
            participant: participant,
            work: work,
            key: key,
            started_at: now(),
            threshold: perceptibility_threshold(),
            ended: false,
            outcome: null
        )

    @guidance
        -- The wait begins when the work begins, not when someone notices it is
        -- slow. A wait started late reports a duration shorter than the one the
        -- participant lived through, which is the same as understating it.
}

rule ShowElapsedOnceItIsPerceptible {
    when: WaitObserved(wait)

    requires: wait.ended = false
    requires: now() - wait.started_at >= wait.threshold

    ensures:
        ElapsedIndication.created(
            wait: wait,
            elapsed: now() - wait.started_at,
            usually_takes: usual_duration_for(wait.key)
        )

    @guidance
        -- The elapsed value is recomputed often enough that the displayed number
        -- never disagrees with a clock the participant can also see.
}

rule WithholdIndicationForBriefWork {
    when: WaitObserved(wait)

    requires: now() - wait.started_at < wait.threshold

    ensures: not exists ElapsedIndication{wait: wait}
}

rule StopSayingTheUsualOnceItIsPast {
    when: indication: ElapsedIndication.created

    requires: indication.usually_takes != null
    requires: indication.elapsed >= tolerance_of(indication.usually_takes)

    ensures: indication.usually_takes = null

    @guidance
        -- Repeating "usually takes 20 s" at the fortieth second is Vera insisting on
        -- something that has stopped being true, and a participant reads it as being
        -- mocked. From there only the count remains, which is what there is.
}

rule RememberWhatTheWorkTook {
    when: WorkEnded(wait, outcome)

    requires: wait.key != null
    requires: outcome = succeeded
    requires: now() - wait.started_at >= wait.threshold

    ensures:
        WaitMemory{key: wait.key, measured_on: wait.participant}.durations = most_recent(
            WaitMemory{key: wait.key, measured_on: wait.participant}.durations
                + (now() - wait.started_at)
        )

    @guidance
        -- Only what succeeded. Work that failed at the second second did not take
        -- two seconds to do; it took two seconds to fail, and remembering it as the
        -- former would promise a speed Vera cannot deliver.
        --
        -- The expectation offered to a participant is the middle value of what was
        -- measured and not the average. One call that went to ninety seconds because
        -- the machine was busy must not change what is promised the next twenty
        -- times. The middle value ignores it; the average drags it along.
}

rule DoNotRememberAnUnnamedWait {
    when: WorkEnded(wait, outcome)

    requires: wait.key = null

    ensures: not exists WaitMemory{key: wait.work}

    @guidance
        -- Counting always, remembering only what can be named. Between two known
        -- milestones, what the machine is doing is the observer's conjecture; stored
        -- as a measurement it would make Vera promise "usually takes 12 s" about a
        -- wait that was for something else.
}

rule EndTheIndicationWithTheWork {
    when: WorkEnded(wait, outcome)

    ensures:
        wait.ended = true
        wait.outcome = outcome
        not exists ElapsedIndication{wait: wait}
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Invariants
------------------------------------------------------------

invariant EveryIndicationBelongsToUnfinishedWork {
    for indication in ElapsedIndications:
        indication.wait.ended = false
}

invariant AnIndicationNeverShowsLessThanTheThreshold {
    for indication in ElapsedIndications:
        indication.elapsed >= indication.wait.threshold
}

invariant RememberedDurationsAreNamed {
    for memory in WaitMemories:
        memory.key != null
}

invariant ElapsedIsTimeThatPassedAndNotTimeRemaining {
    for indication in ElapsedIndications:
        indication.elapsed = now() - indication.wait.started_at
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Actors and surfaces
------------------------------------------------------------

actor WaitingParticipant {
    within: core/Graph
    identified_by: core/Participant
}

surface WaitIndication {
    facing participant: WaitingParticipant
    context wait: Wait

    exposes:
        wait.work
        wait.started_at
        wait.outcome

    provides:
        WorkBegan(participant, work, key?)
        WaitObserved(wait)
        WorkEnded(wait, outcome)

    contracts:
        fulfils TruthfulWaiting

    @guarantee TheLongestWaitIsTheOneMostWorthShowing
        -- Wherever Vera hands work to something slower than itself — a model
        -- transcribing speech, a query crossing the whole corpus, a document being
        -- imported, a page arriving from the server — that is where the count
        -- belongs. A wait that is already fast does not need one.

    @guarantee AnUnknownWaitBecomesAnExpectedOne
        -- Almost all of the discomfort of waiting is not knowing whether the wait is
        -- normal. Saying what this device measured before converts an unknown wait
        -- into an expected one without predicting anything.

    @guarantee TheCountIsWhereTheGestureWas
        -- The count appears where the participant last acted or where the result
        -- will appear, and not in a corner reserved for machine status. Someone who
        -- pressed a button looks at the button.

    @guarantee ConcurrentWorkHasOnePeripheralSignal
        -- One or several simultaneous waits produce one shared frame around the
        -- interface. It remains until the last wait ends, while each wait keeps
        -- its own name, elapsed count and available explanation at its origin.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open Questions
------------------------------------------------------------

open question "Should measured durations travel between a participant's devices, given that a phone and a workstation calling the same local model do not measure the same thing?"

open question "When a wait exceeds what it usually takes by a large factor, is there a point at which Vera should offer to abandon it rather than only continue counting?"

open question "Work composed of many named steps can be counted per step or end to end. Which of the two does a participant actually want to see, and does the answer differ between work they are watching and work they left running?"
